# `<predicate> of <name>` — ouroboros#119.
#
# THE POINT OF THIS FIXTURE is the multi-channel case. Bare predicates read the
# LAST-OBSERVED binding, so in a loop that assigns several observed variables
# per iteration they can only ever answer about the last one. The named form is
# the only correct form here, and it did not compile at all before #119.
#
# `a` climbs steadily, `b` oscillates, `c` is pinned. Asked BY NAME, the three
# must give three different answers; asked bare, all three questions would be
# about `c` alone.
a is 100.0
b is 5.0
c is 7.0
i is 0
loop while i < 14:
    a is a + 10.0
    b is 0.0 - b
    c is 7.0
    i is i + 1

print of ("a.dive=" + (str of (diverging of a)))
print of ("a.osci=" + (str of (oscillating of a)))
print of ("b.osci=" + (str of (oscillating of b)))
print of ("b.dive=" + (str of (diverging of b)))
print of ("c.stab=" + (str of (stable of c)))
print of ("c.osci=" + (str of (oscillating of c)))

# the named form in a CONDITION and in arithmetic, not only as a print operand
n is 0
if diverging of a:
    n is n + 1
if oscillating of b:
    n is n + 10
n is n + (stable of c)
print of ("n=" + (str of n))

# A binding assigned once and never moved. This row pins that the answer is 0
# rather than a confident verdict — but NOT, as an earlier version of this
# comment claimed, because of the named ops' `used` gate: the bare form answers
# 0 here too, so the predicate itself is what makes it false and flipping
# require_used 1->0 leaves this fixture byte-identical (mechanical-gates §41 —
# a row that passes for a different reason than its comment states).
#
# The `used` gate is genuinely exercised UPSTREAM, where the VM's bare and
# named opcodes differ on an in-range never-assigned slot; it is not
# discriminated by anything in this file, and that is recorded rather than
# papered over.
z is 3.0
print of ("z.equi=" + (str of (equilibrium of z)))
